    text: "An individual retirement account, or IRA, is an investment account you open yourself with a bank or other financial institution, a life insurance company, a mutual fund company or a stockbroker, which gives you a choice of investment options, typically mutual funds. A traditional IRA may give you a tax deduction, and earnings are not taxed until you withdraw. A Roth IRA gives no deduction, but qualified withdrawals are tax-free. For 2026 the IRA contribution limit, traditional and Roth combined, is $7,500, or $8,600 if you are 50 or older, or your taxable pay if that is less. The income ranges for 2026: a Roth contribution is reduced for a single person or head of household between $153,000 and $168,000 of income, and the deduction for a traditional IRA when you are covered by a workplace plan is reduced for a single person between $81,000 and $91,000. You can make contributions for a tax year until the tax filing date in April of the next year, and an excess contribution faces a 6 percent tax each year unless you remove it by the return due date. Money taken out before age 59 and a half generally faces an extra 10 percent tax on top of income tax, unless an exception applies. The Saver's Credit gives 50, 20 or 10 percent of up to $2,000 of contributions, or $4,000 if married filing jointly, for people 18 or older who are not full-time students or claimed as dependents; the 2026 income cutoffs are $40,250 for a single person, $60,375 for head of household and $80,500 for a married couple filing jointly. One distinction matters: an IRA is an account, and a fund is an investment held inside it. The SEC's cash sweep bulletin says uninvested cash in an investment account sits in a sweep program, often a bank deposit by default, until you choose an investment, so opening or funding an account does not by itself invest the money. I could not read the IRS pages on IRA investments or FINRA's pages."
